    classCard({
      name: "Calcium channel blocker overdose physiology and rescue pathway",
      generic: "calcium channel blocker overdose physiology and rescue pathway",
      aliases: ["CCB overdose", "calcium channel blocker toxicity", "amlodipine overdose", "verapamil overdose", "diltiazem overdose", "why high dose insulin for calcium blocker overdose", "CCB poisoning antidote"],
      class: "Toxicologic vasoplegic, cardiogenic, conduction, and metabolic shock syndrome",
      classExampleNames: ["Amlodipine", "Diltiazem", "Verapamil"],
      classExampleKeys: ["amlodipine", "diltiazem", "verapamil"],
      classPathway: ["Toxicology", "Excess L-type calcium-channel blockade", "Calcium, high-dose insulin, vasopressors, and advanced shock support"],
      usedToTreat: "Recognizing and organizing emergency care for potentially toxic calcium-channel-blocker exposure. Any suspected significant ingestion requires immediate emergency and poison-center evaluation; this card explains physiology and team-directed treatment rather than providing a self-treatment plan.",
      description: "Calcium channel blocker overdose can simultaneously remove arterial tone, suppress SA/AV-node conduction, weaken myocardial contraction, and block pancreatic beta-cell insulin release. Dihydropyridines often begin with profound vasodilatory shock and reflex tachycardia, but selectivity disappears at large exposures and bradycardia or myocardial depression can follow. Verapamil and diltiazem more often cause early bradycardia, AV block, and cardiogenic shock. Hyperglycemia is an important clue because calcium-dependent insulin secretion falls while shock creates insulin resistance and the stressed myocardium cannot use glucose efficiently. Extended-release products and long-half-life amlodipine can delay or prolong collapse, so a well-appearing early patient is not automatically safe.",
      mechanism: "Excess Cav1.2 blockade reduces smooth-muscle calcium-calmodulin signaling, causing vasoplegia, and reduces cardiomyocyte trigger calcium, causing low contractility. Nodal calcium-current suppression slows automaticity and AV conduction. In pancreatic beta cells, membrane depolarization normally opens L-type channels and calcium triggers insulin-granule exocytosis; blockade therefore lowers insulin release, producing hyperglycemia and limiting myocardial carbohydrate uptake. High-dose insulin with dextrose bypasses that endocrine failure, increases myocardial glucose transport and oxidation, improves calcium handling and inotropy, and can restore cardiac output even when ordinary vasopressors are insufficient. IV calcium raises the extracellular gradient and can partially overcome channel blockade; norepinephrine/epinephrine support vascular tone and contractility. Refractory cardiogenic shock may require lipid emulsion in selected cases or VA-ECMO while the drug clears.",
      boxedWarning: "Life-threatening poisoning warning: call emergency services and a poison center immediately for suspected significant ingestion, dosing error, or unexplained shock/bradycardia in a patient taking a CCB. Do not wait for symptoms and do not induce vomiting. First-line expert-directed therapies can include IV calcium, early high-dose insulin with dextrose, and norepinephrine and/or epinephrine according to the hemodynamic phenotype. High-dose insulin requires intensive glucose, potassium, fluid, and hemodynamic monitoring because severe hypoglycemia, hypokalemia, and volume overload can occur. Pacing may restore electrical rate but fail to generate output when myocardium is profoundly depressed; treatment must address both conduction and pump failure.",
      adverseEffects: ["Hypotension, vasoplegia, bradycardia or tachycardia, AV block, junctional rhythm, myocardial depression, pulmonary edema, altered mental status, seizures, ileus, lactic acidosis, hyperglycemia, kidney/liver injury, and cardiac arrest.", "Treatment hazards include hypercalcemia, extravasation, catecholamine arrhythmia/ischemia, high-dose-insulin hypoglycemia/hypokalemia/volume load, lipid-emulsion complications, and invasive-support complications."],
      contraindications: ["There is no home antidote and no role for routine outpatient observation after a potentially toxic exposure. Decontamination, observation length, and treatment depend on formulation, time, amount, co-ingestants, and symptoms.", "Hemodialysis does not meaningfully enhance elimination of highly protein-bound amlodipine, diltiazem, or verapamil; do not delay hemodynamic rescue while pursuing ineffective clearance."],
      nursingEssentials: ["Obtain drug, strength, formulation, amount, time, co-ingestants, weight, symptoms, and baseline cardiac/renal/hepatic history. Bring packaging when safe. Establish continuous ECG, frequent pressure, IV access, glucose, electrolytes, gas/lactate, renal/hepatic studies, urine output, and bedside cardiac assessment.", "During high-dose insulin, follow the toxicology protocol for insulin/dextrose delivery, bedside glucose at short intervals, potassium and magnesium trends, fluid concentration/volume, pump safety, and gradual weaning only after hemodynamics remain stable."],
      interactions: ["Beta blockers, digoxin, clonidine, antiarrhythmics, sedatives, and other antihypertensives can deepen bradycardia, conduction block, myocardial depression, or hypotension.", "CYP3A inhibitors or hepatic impairment can raise chronic CCB exposure and convert a usual regimen into toxicity; extended-release formulations and amlodipine's long half-life prolong risk."],
      keyLabs: ["Serial glucose, potassium, magnesium, ionized calcium, bicarbonate/pH, lactate, creatinine, liver tests, urine output, ECG intervals/rhythm, and bedside echocardiographic contractility.", "During antidotal/supportive infusions, trend response to therapy rather than one number: mean pressure, pulse pressure, perfusion, cardiac output surrogate, vasopressor dose, lactate clearance, mental status, and urine output."],
      nclexTraps: ["Hyperglycemia supports CCB poisoning because pancreatic calcium blockade suppresses insulin release; beta-blocker poisoning more often lacks this strong hyperglycemic clue, although overlap occurs.", "Calcium is not the entire antidote plan. Severe poisoning commonly requires high-dose insulin and vasopressors, and electrical pacing may not create mechanical perfusion in a profoundly depressed heart."],
      sourceNote: "AHA poisoning update and adult expert consensus: https://professional.heart.org/en/science-news/2023-american-heart-association-focused-update-on-the-management-of-patients-with-cardiac-arrest/top-things-to-know ; https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/27749343/ ; EXTRIP recommendation: https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/33555964/",
      tags: ["frontier-wave13", "CCB overdose", "calcium channel blocker toxicity", "high dose insulin", "euglycemia", "hyperglycemia", "vasoplegia", "cardiogenic shock", "calcium", "VA ECMO"]
    })
